    "case_id": "268",
    "case_summary": "A 66-year-old man presented with a 24-hour history of incomplete closure of the right eye, flattening of the right nasolabial fold, and right-sided hemiplegia rendering him unable to sit up or stand. The patient had a 3-year history of hypertension but no history of neurological disorders. The patient was alert and oriented.\n\n- Effacement of the right frontal wrinkle- Incomplete closure of the right eyelid- Flattening of the right nasolabial fold- Muscle strength in the right upper and lower extremities: grade 0/5- Hypoesthesia noted on the right side- Right Babinski sign: positive\n\n- Imaging Studies:  1. Emergency CT scan of the head:     Image Title: Cranial computed tomography     Image Description: Hemorrhage in the left thalamus-radiation crown region and in the lateral ventricle at 24 hours postonset    2. Cerebral MRI with susceptibility-weighted imaging (1 week and 1 month after onset):     Image Description: No definitive lesions in the brainstem or evidence of hemorrhage-related iron deposition in the ventral part of the pons    3. Cerebral MRI with diffusion tensor imaging (1 month after onset):     Image Description: Corticobulbar tract appeared thinner on the left side than on the right before entering the brainstem",
